WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.662 --> 00:00:03.370
(swooshing)

00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:05.410
- Good morning, Believe
Nation, I'm Evan Carmichael,

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:08.547
my One Word is Believe, and
I believe that entrepreneurs

00:00:08.547 --> 00:00:12.065
are going to solve all of
the world's major problems.

00:00:12.065 --> 00:00:15.313
I started the Believe Life
Series to try to step outside

00:00:15.313 --> 00:00:16.894
just the world of entrepreneurship,

00:00:16.894 --> 00:00:20.198
and learn from different
areas to try to help us become

00:00:20.198 --> 00:00:22.627
better, more well rounded individuals

00:00:22.627 --> 00:00:24.736
who can go out and have a big impact.

00:00:24.736 --> 00:00:26.521
So in today's episode,
we're going to learn

00:00:26.521 --> 00:00:28.985
how you can travel the world.

00:00:28.985 --> 00:00:30.665
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:30.665 --> 00:00:33.043
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:33.043 --> 00:00:34.680
And always guys, as you're watching,

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.992
if someone says something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:36.992 --> 00:00:38.764
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:38.764 --> 00:00:41.352
and put quotes around it so
other people can be inspired.

00:00:41.352 --> 00:00:43.317
And when you write it down for yourself,

00:00:43.317 --> 00:00:47.656
it's much more likely to
stick for you as well, enjoy.

00:00:47.656 --> 00:00:50.239
(bouncy music)

00:01:09.085 --> 00:01:13.252
- What is it about new places
that attracts us to them?

00:01:14.711 --> 00:01:18.294
We thirst to wander,
explore, and discover.

00:01:20.001 --> 00:01:24.168
What drives us to fly around
the world to touch a wall?

00:01:25.787 --> 00:01:28.787
To find our likes, and our dislikes?

00:01:31.537 --> 00:01:34.620
It's in our veins, it's human nature,

00:01:36.813 --> 00:01:38.730
that drives us farther,

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:44.335
that pushes us higher,

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:51.817
that shows us who we
are and breaks us free.

00:01:53.378 --> 00:01:56.545
(inspirational music)

00:02:19.478 --> 00:02:23.645
Once you've touched the world,
you've changed yours forever.

00:02:24.746 --> 00:02:27.413
(kids laughing)

00:02:30.545 --> 00:02:33.795
It's a craving that exists in all of us

00:02:35.166 --> 00:02:38.333
which leaves you with only one option,

00:02:42.075 --> 00:02:43.325
pick a place...

00:02:44.402 --> 00:02:46.402
Wow, that looks amazing.

00:02:47.996 --> 00:02:50.205
You buy a ticket...

00:02:50.205 --> 00:02:53.526
- Can I book a one way
ticket to Thailand please?

00:02:53.526 --> 00:02:55.776
- [Kristen] You pack a bag,

00:02:58.879 --> 00:02:59.879
make a plan,

00:03:01.713 --> 00:03:03.130
and you go again.

00:03:53.693 --> 00:03:56.943
(high pitched chiming)

00:03:58.153 --> 00:04:00.351
- If you have a dream, if
you have this crazy idea,

00:04:00.351 --> 00:04:04.719
you must believe in it
even if no one else does.

00:04:04.719 --> 00:04:07.930
So when I had the idea to go
to every country in the world,

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:09.697
that's kind of what it felt like,

00:04:09.697 --> 00:04:11.158
it felt like this crazy idea.

00:04:11.158 --> 00:04:13.006
It was like, this just sounds
like totally ridiculous

00:04:13.006 --> 00:04:15.041
and absurd, but I couldn't
stop thinking about it.

00:04:15.041 --> 00:04:18.297
And I realized that if I didn't pursue it,

00:04:18.297 --> 00:04:20.577
I would always regret it.

00:04:20.577 --> 00:04:23.397
And that knowledge, that fear of regret,

00:04:23.397 --> 00:04:25.362
kind of drove me to say yes.

00:04:25.362 --> 00:04:26.591
And I talked to lots of other people

00:04:26.591 --> 00:04:28.369
who used very similar language,

00:04:28.369 --> 00:04:30.299
there's a guy from Portland, Maine

00:04:30.299 --> 00:04:33.539
whose crazy idea was
to walk across America,

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:35.368
and he wasn't even sure
how does that work?

00:04:35.368 --> 00:04:36.445
How much time does it take?

00:04:36.445 --> 00:04:37.888
Has anyone ever done this before?

00:04:37.888 --> 00:04:40.334
It was this crazy idea that
wouldn't leave him alone.

00:04:40.334 --> 00:04:42.530
And so one morning, he
left his home in Maine,

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:45.760
and he started walking, and
seven and a half months later

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:48.514
he arrived in San Francisco.

00:04:48.514 --> 00:04:50.532
And I talked to him
throughout the whole journey,

00:04:50.532 --> 00:04:51.846
I talked to him at the
beginning, and the middle

00:04:51.846 --> 00:04:54.589
and the end, and I asked him
what were the challenges like?

00:04:54.589 --> 00:04:56.782
How did it go, and he said,

00:04:56.782 --> 00:04:58.032
he said something that stayed with me

00:04:58.032 --> 00:04:59.587
for the rest of my journey,

00:04:59.587 --> 00:05:03.314
he said, you know, there are
lots of external challenges.

00:05:03.314 --> 00:05:06.431
It was hard on the body to
get used to walking that much,

00:05:06.431 --> 00:05:07.744
lots of things went wrong.

00:05:07.744 --> 00:05:09.875
There were some emotional
challenges as well,

00:05:09.875 --> 00:05:11.774
he just had a breakup, and he was kind of

00:05:11.774 --> 00:05:13.278
processing all of this.

00:05:13.278 --> 00:05:16.135
He said what I realized
about two weeks in,

00:05:16.135 --> 00:05:18.655
once I stopped complaining and grumbling

00:05:18.655 --> 00:05:22.003
about the whole thing,
he said I realized that

00:05:22.003 --> 00:05:24.321
my task was pretty simple.

00:05:24.321 --> 00:05:26.538
All I had to do was walk, essentially,

00:05:26.538 --> 00:05:29.067
and if I did nothing else,
if I got up every morning

00:05:29.067 --> 00:05:31.291
and I made a little bit of progress,

00:05:31.291 --> 00:05:33.507
then eventually I would reach the goal.

00:05:33.507 --> 00:05:35.310
Eventually I would
arrive in San Francisco,

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:38.424
and achieve that goal,
reach that destination.

00:05:38.424 --> 00:05:40.096
And it sounded really simple.

00:05:40.096 --> 00:05:41.664
But it's kind of stayed with me

00:05:41.664 --> 00:05:44.824
for the final two years of
my quest because that's how

00:05:44.824 --> 00:05:46.344
I felt it was for me too.

00:05:46.344 --> 00:05:48.378
All I had to do was get to 193 countries,

00:05:48.378 --> 00:05:50.058
and I had my list of countries.

00:05:50.058 --> 00:05:53.185
And I was making progress,
and if I got stuck somewhere,

00:05:53.185 --> 00:05:54.979
eventually I'd have to
overcome that challenge

00:05:54.979 --> 00:05:56.659
but I could go and do some others first.

00:05:56.659 --> 00:06:00.556
And I liked the logical,
analytical progress of it.

00:06:00.556 --> 00:06:03.111
It's something that related
to me so, just keep going,

00:06:03.111 --> 00:06:04.174
you have to believe in your dream

00:06:04.174 --> 00:06:05.549
even if no one else does.

00:06:05.549 --> 00:06:07.925
- [Caty] Let me give you the
basics of what I personally did

00:06:07.925 --> 00:06:10.791
to travel for nearly three
months, financially speaking.

00:06:10.791 --> 00:06:13.836
First I picked my destination
and bought my plane tickets.

00:06:13.836 --> 00:06:16.127
In this particular case, it
was actually cheaper for me

00:06:16.127 --> 00:06:18.764
to buy a one way ticket to New York City

00:06:18.764 --> 00:06:21.327
and then a one way ticket
from New York to Berlin

00:06:21.327 --> 00:06:24.235
with a stopover in Oslo
than it was for me to buy

00:06:24.235 --> 00:06:27.377
a one way ticket directly
from Las Vegas to Berlin

00:06:27.377 --> 00:06:28.563
with any stopovers.

00:06:28.563 --> 00:06:30.605
So although it added a little bit of time

00:06:30.605 --> 00:06:33.595
to my itinerary, I saved
a couple hundred dollars.

00:06:33.595 --> 00:06:37.340
My favorite website for buying
tickets is Google.com/flights

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:40.872
because it shows you both on
a map and in calendar format

00:06:40.872 --> 00:06:43.331
the specific days as well as the airports

00:06:43.331 --> 00:06:45.586
it would be cheapest to fly into.

00:06:45.586 --> 00:06:48.154
If you're somebody who always
goes for the cheapest flights,

00:06:48.154 --> 00:06:50.731
this is the best tool that
I have personally found.

00:06:50.731 --> 00:06:52.431
And if you're the more adventurous type

00:06:52.431 --> 00:06:55.661
with no specific destination
in mind, skyscanner.com

00:06:55.661 --> 00:06:58.671
is a fantastic option simply
because they literally

00:06:58.671 --> 00:07:01.457
have the option to search
for anywhere on the globe.

00:07:01.457 --> 00:07:05.457
So for example, let's say
that you are available

00:07:05.457 --> 00:07:07.660
at some point during the month of March,

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.642
but you don't know when
yet, and you're available

00:07:09.642 --> 00:07:12.480
to go anywhere but you don't
know where you want to go.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:15.688
On Skyscanner, you can
literally select an entire month

00:07:15.688 --> 00:07:18.265
that you would want to
travel at some point during,

00:07:18.265 --> 00:07:21.226
and you can actually select
the Everywhere option

00:07:21.226 --> 00:07:22.907
when you're searching for a destination.

00:07:22.907 --> 00:07:26.111
So doing it this way will
show you the cheapest

00:07:26.111 --> 00:07:30.446
place to fly on the planet
during the entire month of March

00:07:30.446 --> 00:07:33.125
to travel to from your
specific destination.

00:07:33.125 --> 00:07:36.064
So if you're the adventurous
type, I highly recommend,

00:07:36.064 --> 00:07:37.071
it's fantastic.

00:07:37.071 --> 00:07:39.371
Once I bought my plane
tickets, I researched

00:07:39.371 --> 00:07:40.834
a little bit of what I wanted to do

00:07:40.834 --> 00:07:43.056
in the specific countries I was going to.

00:07:43.056 --> 00:07:46.582
For me, I personally love
museums, learning about history,

00:07:46.582 --> 00:07:48.515
and the occasional nice meal.

00:07:48.515 --> 00:07:50.570
I also knew that I would
be traveling by train

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:52.718
between countries, and I
would be staying primarily

00:07:52.718 --> 00:07:55.847
in hostels, so I pretty much just Googled

00:07:55.847 --> 00:07:58.337
a little bit of what I
wanted to do in each country,

00:07:58.337 --> 00:08:00.410
got a price range for
those specific activities,

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:03.022
added it up and multiplied
it by the number of days

00:08:03.022 --> 00:08:04.102
I would be traveling.

00:08:04.102 --> 00:08:05.669
This was my basic budget.

00:08:05.669 --> 00:08:07.863
Due to the fact that I'd
be traveling through Europe

00:08:07.863 --> 00:08:10.406
during the high season,
it can get expensive.

00:08:10.406 --> 00:08:14.573
So just to be safe, I upped
my daily budget to $100 a day.

00:08:15.509 --> 00:08:19.045
Yes, this is a lot of money,
no you don't need to do this.

00:08:19.045 --> 00:08:23.362
I ended up spending, on
average, way under $100 a day.

00:08:23.362 --> 00:08:25.453
But it was comforting
knowing that I had a little

00:08:25.453 --> 00:08:28.527
extra wiggle room in my budget
in case anything came up.

00:08:28.527 --> 00:08:31.504
I highly recommend giving
yourself a daily budget,

00:08:31.504 --> 00:08:34.135
no matter what it is,
during the entire time

00:08:34.135 --> 00:08:35.214
that you're traveling.

00:08:35.214 --> 00:08:37.945
A daily budget, in my personal
opinion is a lot easier

00:08:37.945 --> 00:08:39.453
to keep in the back of your head,

00:08:39.453 --> 00:08:42.102
than an overarching budget that spans

00:08:42.102 --> 00:08:44.339
for weeks and weeks and weeks on end.

00:08:44.339 --> 00:08:47.360
- For water when we went
to Central America or Asia,

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.528
we used to buy a lot of bottled water,

00:08:50.528 --> 00:08:53.786
and we felt really bad about
it, it was a lot of plastic,

00:08:53.786 --> 00:08:55.691
and it was actually really expensive.

00:08:55.691 --> 00:08:59.186
So now we got ourselves
a really small portable

00:08:59.186 --> 00:09:01.885
water purifier, this one's a Steripen.

00:09:01.885 --> 00:09:04.933
You can charge it with USB
and it works really well.

00:09:04.933 --> 00:09:08.032
It's just an ultraviolet
light that kills the bacteria.

00:09:08.032 --> 00:09:11.435
And we basically just fill up
our water bottle with water,

00:09:11.435 --> 00:09:14.633
you turn it on, put it in here and stir it

00:09:14.633 --> 00:09:16.897
for about a minute, and your water's clean

00:09:16.897 --> 00:09:18.636
and ready to drink.

00:09:18.636 --> 00:09:20.448
- For food and snacks,
we usually tried to bring

00:09:20.448 --> 00:09:22.257
our own stuff because food at the airport

00:09:22.257 --> 00:09:25.081
and the train station, and
basically any touristy area

00:09:25.081 --> 00:09:27.798
is usually overpriced
and really expensive.

00:09:27.798 --> 00:09:29.683
So we'll usually pack
a bag of our own snacks

00:09:29.683 --> 00:09:32.135
so that we can save
money, and also make sure

00:09:32.135 --> 00:09:34.567
that we have the food that
we actually like to eat.

00:09:34.567 --> 00:09:36.157
It's important to check out
the different guidelines

00:09:36.157 --> 00:09:37.911
for where you're going because
they're always changing

00:09:37.911 --> 00:09:40.314
and often you can't bring
things like produce,

00:09:40.314 --> 00:09:42.901
liquids, raw nuts and things like that.

00:09:42.901 --> 00:09:44.937
Packing our food is also
a great way to make sure

00:09:44.937 --> 00:09:46.462
that we're eating healthy
food which we can't

00:09:46.462 --> 00:09:47.808
always find when we're on the move.

00:09:47.808 --> 00:09:51.180
- For staying clean we have
a few really simple things

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:53.801
that we use and that
work really well for us.

00:09:53.801 --> 00:09:57.746
One of our favorite things
is this laundry soap bar.

00:09:57.746 --> 00:10:00.010
We use this to wash our clothes by hand.

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:02.570
It is nice to be independent
and be able to do that

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:03.969
almost wherever you are.

00:10:03.969 --> 00:10:05.374
And it saves us a lot of money

00:10:05.374 --> 00:10:07.113
'cause we don't have to go to laundromats.

00:10:07.113 --> 00:10:10.840
Another thing we carry around
with us is dry shampoo.

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:13.489
I was very skeptical of this when Danielle

00:10:13.489 --> 00:10:15.369
first talked about it
but it actually works

00:10:15.369 --> 00:10:16.683
really, really well.

00:10:16.683 --> 00:10:18.540
It's just a little powder
that you put in your hand,

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:22.597
you mix it around in
your hair, and it's like

00:10:22.597 --> 00:10:24.840
you washed your hair,
you kind of get a little

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:27.165
refresher in between showers.

00:10:27.165 --> 00:10:29.116
You can buy this, but
you can also look it up

00:10:29.116 --> 00:10:31.452
and make your own using
pretty simple ingredients

00:10:31.452 --> 00:10:32.707
like corn starch.

00:10:32.707 --> 00:10:34.161
And another thing that we bring with us

00:10:34.161 --> 00:10:36.416
almost all the time and it helps us deal

00:10:36.416 --> 00:10:39.603
with those days in between
showers are wet wipes.

00:10:39.603 --> 00:10:42.189
We obviously don't bring
a big container like this.

00:10:42.189 --> 00:10:44.888
But wet wipes combined
with the dry shampoo

00:10:44.888 --> 00:10:49.103
are a really great simple,
portable way to stay clean

00:10:49.103 --> 00:10:51.628
on long travel days and between showers.

00:10:51.628 --> 00:10:56.015
And last, we always bring
our trusty Dr Bronners soap.

00:10:56.015 --> 00:10:58.295
This can be used for
anything, it's natural,

00:10:58.295 --> 00:11:01.711
it's unscented, so we can wash dishes,

00:11:01.711 --> 00:11:03.937
we can shower with this.

00:11:03.937 --> 00:11:05.699
So it's really, really handy.

00:11:05.699 --> 00:11:07.284
If you're traveling outside the country,

00:11:07.284 --> 00:11:09.345
you're obviously going
to need your passport,

00:11:09.345 --> 00:11:12.585
and you're going to need to
take really good care of it.

00:11:12.585 --> 00:11:16.011
We got ours stolen once
and we just had one

00:11:16.011 --> 00:11:18.493
that was ruined by water damage.

00:11:18.493 --> 00:11:21.963
And if there's just a bit of water that

00:11:21.963 --> 00:11:24.749
smudges the picture, it's invalid.

00:11:24.749 --> 00:11:27.031
So you got to take really good care of it

00:11:27.031 --> 00:11:29.573
because if something happens
when you're in another country,

00:11:29.573 --> 00:11:32.131
then it's really
expensive to get a new one

00:11:32.131 --> 00:11:33.570
and it's a lot of trouble.

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:36.051
So what we do now is that
we always carry it in

00:11:36.051 --> 00:11:38.309
a traveling pouch,

00:11:38.309 --> 00:11:43.292
sometimes in a pouch that
we can hide on our selves,

00:11:43.292 --> 00:11:46.013
and in that pouch we also keep

00:11:46.013 --> 00:11:48.738
the passport in a Ziploc bag.

00:11:48.738 --> 00:11:51.484
It's really simple, it costs nothing.

00:11:51.484 --> 00:11:54.584
And it'll save it from
getting water damage.

00:11:54.584 --> 00:11:56.134
- Number four, work remotely.

00:11:56.134 --> 00:11:58.891
These days certain jobs
can be done from anywhere.

00:11:58.891 --> 00:12:00.975
Whether it's programming
to graphic design,

00:12:00.975 --> 00:12:03.065
or freelance writing and photography.

00:12:03.065 --> 00:12:05.090
Websites like Upwork
can help you find work

00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:07.635
as a freelancer or hire other freelancers

00:12:07.635 --> 00:12:09.801
to help you take your show on the road.

00:12:09.801 --> 00:12:12.277
The key is geo arbitrage, earning money

00:12:12.277 --> 00:12:14.106
in a strong currency and spending it

00:12:14.106 --> 00:12:16.568
where life's a little bit
cheaper, think Southeast Asia.

00:12:16.568 --> 00:12:19.113
A good resource to learn
more is Timothy Ferriss'

00:12:19.113 --> 00:12:21.594
4-Hour Workweek, seriously, read the book.

00:12:21.594 --> 00:12:23.942
- Make sure you can make money remotely.

00:12:23.942 --> 00:12:25.369
If you can't make money remotely,

00:12:25.369 --> 00:12:27.714
if that's just not really within
your reach, or your grasp,

00:12:27.714 --> 00:12:30.763
the other very obvious option
is get a job somewhere else.

00:12:30.763 --> 00:12:32.914
Whatever you're doing at home,
somebody else in the world

00:12:32.914 --> 00:12:34.757
somewhere else will have a use for it.

00:12:34.757 --> 00:12:36.604
It doesn't really matter,
you can do that job

00:12:36.604 --> 00:12:38.522
somewhere else, granted
you might get paid less.

00:12:38.522 --> 00:12:41.392
You could work in a hostel
where you get room and board

00:12:41.392 --> 00:12:43.777
and some spending money, and
get to hang out with people

00:12:43.777 --> 00:12:46.173
and do cool activities,
as an example, right?

00:12:46.173 --> 00:12:48.553
- Some examples of people
that we met who are doing this

00:12:48.553 --> 00:12:51.897
lifestyle, living indefinitely
were workers at hostels.

00:12:51.897 --> 00:12:53.878
In Nicaragua we met a handful of friends

00:12:53.878 --> 00:12:56.041
that had been there for
years, like literally,

00:12:56.041 --> 00:12:57.902
two, three, four, five years,

00:12:57.902 --> 00:12:59.729
and that's all they do,
they live on the beach,

00:12:59.729 --> 00:13:02.283
they have this beautiful
lifestyle in paradise.

00:13:02.283 --> 00:13:04.469
Granted, they don't
have a lot of luxuries,

00:13:04.469 --> 00:13:07.361
as you can call it that
we would have back home.

00:13:07.361 --> 00:13:10.068
- They have usually a simpler lifestyle.

00:13:10.068 --> 00:13:13.029
You have somewhat OK wifi,

00:13:13.029 --> 00:13:16.392
you have a room that's nothing grandiose.

00:13:16.392 --> 00:13:18.691
You might not have a house
or a car that you own.

00:13:18.691 --> 00:13:20.626
You have a bungalow on the beach

00:13:20.626 --> 00:13:22.520
and you have a little
scooter to rip around in.

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:24.396
You've got coconuts in
front and you've got

00:13:24.396 --> 00:13:25.646
hammocks on the ocean.

00:13:25.646 --> 00:13:27.294
But that's some people's vacation.

00:13:27.294 --> 00:13:30.405
Some people work year round
to get two weeks of that.

00:13:30.405 --> 00:13:32.787
Why don't you just flip the
switch a little bit, right?

00:13:32.787 --> 00:13:36.063
- I met a girl in Thailand
who essentially had been to

00:13:36.063 --> 00:13:39.005
15 countries, and all
she did was would go,

00:13:39.005 --> 00:13:40.592
work at the hostel for a month or so,

00:13:40.592 --> 00:13:41.803
she'd make a few hundred dollars,

00:13:41.803 --> 00:13:43.769
that would be enough to
take her to the next city,

00:13:43.769 --> 00:13:46.781
next country, she'd work
there, she'd work at a bar.

00:13:46.781 --> 00:13:48.697
- It's not difficult is what we're saying.

00:13:48.697 --> 00:13:51.755
A lot of people do it, a lot
of very young people do it.

00:13:51.755 --> 00:13:53.497
Some people just don't
want to go to university,

00:13:53.497 --> 00:13:54.925
they do that, they figure out, oh (muting)

00:13:54.925 --> 00:13:56.693
a lot of people are doing
this, and it's pretty simple.

00:13:56.693 --> 00:13:59.131
And I met a lot of new friends
and just kind of go with it.

00:13:59.131 --> 00:14:01.270
- Hopefully it's inspired you in some way.

00:14:01.270 --> 00:14:03.685
What we really want to get
out of this is to teach you

00:14:03.685 --> 00:14:07.229
and to help you guys understand
that travel is for everyone.

00:14:07.229 --> 00:14:09.054
You don't have to be rich to travel.

00:14:09.054 --> 00:14:09.893
- No, no, no.

00:14:09.893 --> 00:14:11.983
- You just have to be smart,
you need to understand

00:14:11.983 --> 00:14:14.343
the world of travel because you can do it

00:14:14.343 --> 00:14:15.492
with a small amount of money.

00:14:15.492 --> 00:14:17.944
You can do it in a huge way
with a small amount of money.

00:14:17.944 --> 00:14:20.438
And do it forever if you do it right.

00:14:20.438 --> 00:14:23.372
- You don't have to be rich to travel.

00:14:23.372 --> 00:14:26.402
Actually, all expenses
while you're traveling

00:14:26.402 --> 00:14:28.858
fall into three major categories.

00:14:28.858 --> 00:14:32.593
First is transportation to
get from point A to point B.

00:14:32.593 --> 00:14:35.710
The other thing is
accommodation, and the last

00:14:35.710 --> 00:14:38.217
is everything else,
food, drinks and so on.

00:14:38.217 --> 00:14:41.611
If you minimize those three
expenses to some minimum,

00:14:41.611 --> 00:14:44.789
it can be actually cheaper to travel

00:14:44.789 --> 00:14:46.447
than live in your own city.

00:14:46.447 --> 00:14:48.189
For the next five years
I've been traveling

00:14:48.189 --> 00:14:51.436
around the world with almost no money.

00:14:51.436 --> 00:14:53.020
And this is how I did it.

00:14:53.020 --> 00:14:55.475
First thing, I hitchhiked.

00:14:55.475 --> 00:15:00.189
Apart from being free, apart
from being really fast,

00:15:00.189 --> 00:15:03.672
it allowed me one amazing
thing, to have an adventure

00:15:03.672 --> 00:15:06.223
between point A and point B.

00:15:06.223 --> 00:15:08.853
How many of you guys have ever hitchhiked?

00:15:08.853 --> 00:15:11.065
Maybe, OK, oh quite a lot.

00:15:11.065 --> 00:15:13.101
Why, (laughing) what are you doing?

00:15:13.101 --> 00:15:17.118
OK.
(audience laughing)

00:15:17.118 --> 00:15:20.391
I'm going to play you a short
video called Hitchhiking Guide

00:15:20.391 --> 00:15:24.416
just to tell you a few unwritten
rules about hitchhiking

00:15:24.416 --> 00:15:26.666
and some of my experiences.

00:15:28.023 --> 00:15:31.067
("All My Days" by Alexi Murdoch)

00:15:31.067 --> 00:15:34.219
When it comes to hitchhiking,
everything is about

00:15:34.219 --> 00:15:35.802
using common sense.

00:15:37.462 --> 00:15:40.292
First, to start, you have
to get out of the cities

00:15:40.292 --> 00:15:43.938
or villages or wherever you live and

00:15:43.938 --> 00:15:46.588
(mumbling) so you have
to be on the right road,

00:15:46.588 --> 00:15:48.769
going in the right direction and

00:15:48.769 --> 00:15:50.341
it's really basic things.

00:15:50.341 --> 00:15:54.311
Lesson number two, stick up your thumb.

00:15:54.311 --> 00:15:58.585
Probably most of you
have it so just do this.

00:15:58.585 --> 00:16:02.752
♫ I have been searching
Hitchhiking lesson number 36,

00:16:04.923 --> 00:16:06.340
Look decent like,

00:16:07.381 --> 00:16:08.714
shave and stuff.

00:16:09.814 --> 00:16:13.481
And try to wear some
clean clothes whatever,

00:16:14.847 --> 00:16:16.950
or hide behind your backpack,

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:20.164
so nobody sees what you look like.

00:16:20.164 --> 00:16:21.581
Lesson number 54,

00:16:22.765 --> 00:16:25.421
do not hitchhike during night.

00:16:25.421 --> 00:16:26.921
Be in a good mood.

00:16:28.630 --> 00:16:32.813
It's your choice, you're
hitchhiking, you chose this so

00:16:32.813 --> 00:16:36.980
don't be grumpy, don't be like (whining).

00:16:39.073 --> 00:16:43.073
Lesson number 58, eye
contract with the drivers.

00:16:46.754 --> 00:16:49.227
See, this one nearly stopped.

00:16:49.227 --> 00:16:52.212
Yeah, don't hitchhike alone.

00:16:52.212 --> 00:16:55.322
I have a friend that I hitchhike with.

00:16:55.322 --> 00:16:57.989
Meet Maria Juana, this is sheep.

00:16:59.371 --> 00:17:01.243
Lesson number 21,

00:17:01.243 --> 00:17:03.559
talk with the drivers
when you're in the car

00:17:03.559 --> 00:17:05.936
'cause that's the only
way you can repay them.

00:17:05.936 --> 00:17:08.607
They would like to hear
some interesting stories

00:17:08.607 --> 00:17:10.612
if you have one, yeah?

00:17:10.612 --> 00:17:14.302
If not, just listen to their
story, that's sometimes enough.

00:17:14.302 --> 00:17:16.532
Lesson number nine!

00:17:16.532 --> 00:17:19.699
Fasten your seat belt if you have one.

00:17:21.275 --> 00:17:23.692
Hitchhiking lesson number 62,

00:17:24.597 --> 00:17:27.347
when driver is asleep, take over.

00:17:30.317 --> 00:17:32.448
Be always happy, you know like,

00:17:32.448 --> 00:17:35.853
you're hitchhiking, it's your choice.

00:17:35.853 --> 00:17:38.103
Sometimes you have to wait.

00:17:39.096 --> 00:17:43.263
Be patient and don't expect
people will stop 'cause

00:17:44.491 --> 00:17:47.824
you'll be wrong at 99.74% of cases.

00:17:50.416 --> 00:17:52.846
But, no matter how long you wait,

00:17:52.846 --> 00:17:55.429
the right driver will come, so,

00:17:56.390 --> 00:17:58.749
that's the ultimate hitchhiking rule,

00:17:58.749 --> 00:18:01.057
and that's all you have to know, man.

00:18:01.057 --> 00:18:03.791
There are no rules actually,
(bleep) all the rules.

00:18:03.791 --> 00:18:04.624
So...

00:18:06.656 --> 00:18:09.947
Yeah go out there, enjoy your life and

00:18:09.947 --> 00:18:14.876
challenge yourself, eh?
♫ Feels all right

00:18:14.876 --> 00:18:19.043
♫ Now even breathing feels all right

00:18:20.175 --> 00:18:23.258
♫ Yes even breathing

00:18:26.088 --> 00:18:28.255
(honking)

00:18:29.155 --> 00:18:31.988
♫ Feels all right

00:18:36.243 --> 00:18:37.602
When it comes to accommodation

00:18:37.602 --> 00:18:39.195
most of the time I use Couchsurfing

00:18:39.195 --> 00:18:42.422
because I had a lot of
experience, I had a lot of these

00:18:42.422 --> 00:18:45.151
positive references on
the website and so on,

00:18:45.151 --> 00:18:48.234
and what I like about
Couchsurfing the most

00:18:48.234 --> 00:18:51.627
is not only because it's
free, it allows you to

00:18:51.627 --> 00:18:54.310
have a different perspective
of the destination.

00:18:54.310 --> 00:18:57.037
You're not destined to
stay in your hotel room

00:18:57.037 --> 00:19:00.686
or take the tourist tours, you
just hang out with your host,

00:19:00.686 --> 00:19:03.411
he takes you on places
that you would probably

00:19:03.411 --> 00:19:05.046
never visit by yourself.

00:19:05.046 --> 00:19:07.764
In rich cities, in rich
countries, I usually

00:19:07.764 --> 00:19:11.830
buy food in supermarkets
which is the cheapest way

00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:13.869
and just eat on the street.

00:19:13.869 --> 00:19:18.491
You can also cook with your
host which can be pretty

00:19:18.491 --> 00:19:21.932
unique experience to say the least.

00:19:21.932 --> 00:19:24.185
These are Germans actually. (laughing)

00:19:24.185 --> 00:19:26.163
(audience laughing)

00:19:26.163 --> 00:19:27.719
Sorry!

00:19:27.719 --> 00:19:31.638
(laughing) It was pretty
delicious to say the least.

00:19:31.638 --> 00:19:33.833
And one of the cheapest.

00:19:33.833 --> 00:19:36.369
One other thing when it comes to traveling

00:19:36.369 --> 00:19:39.477
is that you can earn money
while you're traveling.

00:19:39.477 --> 00:19:41.956
How to do that? I did it couple of times.

00:19:41.956 --> 00:19:45.022
One of them is busking, playing
the guitar on the street.

00:19:45.022 --> 00:19:47.445
I'm not a musician, I know like probably

00:19:47.445 --> 00:19:50.214
four, five chords and four songs.

00:19:50.214 --> 00:19:52.103
So it's like repeat all,

00:19:52.103 --> 00:19:56.692
people are passing by so
they don't really know.

00:19:56.692 --> 00:19:57.942
(audience laughing)

00:19:57.942 --> 00:20:01.865
But the most important
thing is to have a story.

00:20:01.865 --> 00:20:05.356
I always had my small
card board which I wrote,

00:20:05.356 --> 00:20:07.568
actually somebody else
wrote in the local language

00:20:07.568 --> 00:20:10.694
where I'm from, what I'm
doing there, what's my story.

00:20:10.694 --> 00:20:12.868
And I think that's why people donated

00:20:12.868 --> 00:20:16.693
a little bit of money, some
sandwiches, sodas, and so on.

00:20:16.693 --> 00:20:19.425
You won't earn a lot
of money by doing this,

00:20:19.425 --> 00:20:21.709
but it can get you through the day.

00:20:21.709 --> 00:20:23.860
One other way is to write.

00:20:23.860 --> 00:20:27.613
You can write a blog,
open up a Facebook page,

00:20:27.613 --> 00:20:30.895
after a while you can maybe
write a book, and so on.

00:20:30.895 --> 00:20:32.880
Is it for everyone,
this type of traveling?

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:34.161
I don't think so.

00:20:34.161 --> 00:20:36.947
With all the amazing things that

00:20:36.947 --> 00:20:39.577
this kind of traveling can bring to you,

00:20:39.577 --> 00:20:41.884
there are also downsides.

00:20:41.884 --> 00:20:44.749
It's a big chance that you will be lonely,

00:20:44.749 --> 00:20:48.614
that you will be hungry,
that you will be sick,

00:20:48.614 --> 00:20:50.450
that you will be homesick.

00:20:50.450 --> 00:20:54.292
But, it all comes down
to your gut feeling.

00:20:54.292 --> 00:20:58.247
If, after all these ideas
and all these knowledges,

00:20:58.247 --> 00:21:02.524
you still have that, wow, I
wish I could travel like this,

00:21:02.524 --> 00:21:04.343
then you should definitely do it.

00:21:04.343 --> 00:21:07.182
Then you should forget about your fears,

00:21:07.182 --> 00:21:09.777
disregard the fact that you're broke,

00:21:09.777 --> 00:21:12.405
and just leave, head to the road.

00:21:12.405 --> 00:21:14.801
Because, like that famous quote says,

00:21:14.801 --> 00:21:17.863
in 20 years from now on, you
will be more disappointed

00:21:17.863 --> 00:21:22.030
by the things you didn't do
than by the things you did do.

00:21:23.953 --> 00:21:25.128
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:21:25.128 --> 00:21:27.488
I'd love to know what did
you think of this video?

00:21:27.488 --> 00:21:30.139
What was the biggest
takeaway that you're going to

00:21:30.139 --> 00:21:32.613
immediately apply somehow in your life?

00:21:32.613 --> 00:21:34.736
Or maybe your business, let me know,

00:21:34.736 --> 00:21:36.183
leave it down in the comments below,

00:21:36.183 --> 00:21:37.420
really curious to find out.

00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:39.736
I also wanted to give a quick
shout out to Wesley Lavinder

00:21:39.736 --> 00:21:42.880
from fullyoptimized.org,
Wesley thank you so much

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:45.074
for picking up a copy of
my book, Your One Word,

00:21:45.074 --> 00:21:47.040
and offering to do a review on it,

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:48.797
I really appreciate the support man,

00:21:48.797 --> 00:21:50.321
and I'm glad you're enjoying the read.

00:21:50.321 --> 00:21:51.513
So thank you guys so much for watching.

00:21:51.513 --> 00:21:54.311
I hope you have an amazing
day, I believe in you,

00:21:54.311 --> 00:21:56.306
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself,

00:21:56.306 --> 00:22:01.301
and whatever Your One Word is,
much love, I'll see you soon.

00:22:01.301 --> 00:22:05.384
(swooshing, basketball bouncing)

00:22:07.226 --> 00:22:10.076
- [Woman] We all have
these great ideas, right?

00:22:10.076 --> 00:22:12.435
And at the same time we
have responsibilities

00:22:12.435 --> 00:22:14.333
and mortgages and whatever.

00:22:14.333 --> 00:22:16.267
So I guess I'm just
wondering what your advice is

00:22:16.267 --> 00:22:20.262
to people who want to go out
and pursue certain things

00:22:20.262 --> 00:22:24.212
but there're also certain
limitations I guess

00:22:24.212 --> 00:22:26.625
that we have to exist within.

00:22:26.625 --> 00:22:30.852
I guess we don't, but I
can't walk away from my condo

00:22:30.852 --> 00:22:32.617
tomorrow and walk across the country,

00:22:32.617 --> 00:22:34.654
it probably wouldn't work
out well for me. (laughing)

00:22:34.654 --> 00:22:36.451
- That's a great question,

00:22:36.451 --> 00:22:38.355
but do you want to walk
across the country?

00:22:38.355 --> 00:22:40.535
Is that your aspiration?
(laughing)

00:22:40.535 --> 00:22:43.791
I'm a big fan of defining our aspirations

00:22:43.791 --> 00:22:46.587
and then crafting our life
around them essentially.

00:22:46.587 --> 00:22:49.373
And so like the quest
to visit every country

00:22:49.373 --> 00:22:50.870
in the world for example,
I was thinking about this

00:22:50.870 --> 00:22:53.549
the other day 'cause people
ask how much it cost, right?

00:22:53.549 --> 00:22:57.776
And the first 100 countries
cost about $30,000.

00:22:57.776 --> 00:22:59.718
So 30,000, at least I would argue

00:22:59.718 --> 00:23:02.121
is not a huge amount of money
for going to 100 countries

00:23:02.121 --> 00:23:05.149
and all the life experience
that I got through that.

00:23:05.149 --> 00:23:07.433
And also I didn't have
to pay it all right away.

00:23:07.433 --> 00:23:10.563
You pay as you go, essentially
in something like that.

00:23:10.563 --> 00:23:11.396
So...

00:23:12.533 --> 00:23:14.146
These limitations as you said,

00:23:14.146 --> 00:23:15.448
maybe let's not call them limitations,

00:23:15.448 --> 00:23:16.480
they're responsibilities.

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:18.330
Especially people with
families like you don't just

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:20.754
leave your family and
walk across the country.

00:23:20.754 --> 00:23:23.211
But, again there are ways to cultivate

00:23:23.211 --> 00:23:25.117
the value of adventure,
that's why I like the story

00:23:25.117 --> 00:23:27.158
of Sasha and what she did at home.

00:23:27.158 --> 00:23:29.603
There's a woman in Omaha
who's knitting 10,000 hats.

00:23:29.603 --> 00:23:31.868
And her passion, the thing
that she really loved

00:23:31.868 --> 00:23:34.394
was always knitting, and one
Christmastime or holiday time

00:23:34.394 --> 00:23:37.205
she made 100 hats for
her friends and family.

00:23:37.205 --> 00:23:38.701
And she was like, that's really fun.

00:23:38.701 --> 00:23:41.551
I want to do 1000, but 1000
is not a big enough challenge

00:23:41.551 --> 00:23:43.389
so she took on this goal of 10,000 hats.

00:23:43.389 --> 00:23:45.614
And there's a charity component to it.

00:23:45.614 --> 00:23:46.904
You can connect with her on her website

00:23:46.904 --> 00:23:49.066
and eventually get a hat.

00:23:49.066 --> 00:23:51.268
So, I mean I don't know,
responsibilities and things

00:23:51.268 --> 00:23:54.758
are real, but if something
matters to us, I guess I'm also

00:23:54.758 --> 00:23:57.414
a believer in figuring
out how to do that, right?

00:23:57.414 --> 00:23:58.644
Like we do have to trade off things.

00:23:58.644 --> 00:23:59.679
We do have to sacrifice.

00:23:59.679 --> 00:24:02.676
And especially for all of us
with so many opportunities

00:24:02.676 --> 00:24:06.281
available to us, it's a
matter of prioritization.

00:24:06.281 --> 00:24:07.698
It's a matter of,

00:24:08.718 --> 00:24:11.488
I can probably do anything I
want but I can't do everything.

00:24:11.488 --> 00:24:15.032
Right, so, if this thing
really does matter to me

00:24:15.032 --> 00:24:16.468
whether it's walking across the country

00:24:16.468 --> 00:24:18.563
or whatever it is in your case,

00:24:18.563 --> 00:24:20.482
then it's something that
you have to plan for

00:24:20.482 --> 00:24:23.341
and build for and for me
it was worth it to do that.

00:24:23.341 --> 00:24:26.702
- I have a small story, short
story about these prejudices

00:24:26.702 --> 00:24:29.480
that I encountered while I was traveling.

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:31.230
When I was leaving Croatia and heading

00:24:31.230 --> 00:24:33.657
on my round the world trip,

00:24:33.657 --> 00:24:36.629
everybody was telling me, be careful.

00:24:36.629 --> 00:24:39.063
It's very dangerous,
you're going to hitchhike,

00:24:39.063 --> 00:24:40.913
you're going to sleep
in other peoples' homes,

00:24:40.913 --> 00:24:42.445
and stuff like that.

00:24:42.445 --> 00:24:44.804
In Croatia it's still
OK to travel you know,

00:24:44.804 --> 00:24:48.971
but as soon as you cross the
border, enter into Serbia,

00:24:49.986 --> 00:24:53.468
you know what Serbians are like, you know?

00:24:53.468 --> 00:24:56.359
Be very, very careful,
somebody might kill you.

00:24:56.359 --> 00:24:58.787
And I'm like OK, thank
you for the warning,

00:24:58.787 --> 00:25:01.270
and I crossed the border,
enter into Serbia,

00:25:01.270 --> 00:25:04.066
amazing adventures, amazing people I met,

00:25:04.066 --> 00:25:07.009
people picking me up taking me out,

00:25:07.009 --> 00:25:10.741
sleeping in their homes really,
really amazing experiences.

00:25:10.741 --> 00:25:12.884
And I was leaving Serbia,
heading to Bulgaria,

00:25:12.884 --> 00:25:15.995
I was driving with one
driver and telling him

00:25:15.995 --> 00:25:18.137
that story like how
Croatians were warning me

00:25:18.137 --> 00:25:20.653
about Serbians, and he's like, ah brother,

00:25:20.653 --> 00:25:24.089
that's complete nonsense,
Croatians and Serbians

00:25:24.089 --> 00:25:27.567
we're all brothers, but
Bulgarians, (groaning).

00:25:27.567 --> 00:25:30.249
(audience laughing)

00:25:30.249 --> 00:25:33.436
When you cross the border,
enter into Bulgaria

00:25:33.436 --> 00:25:35.091
you know what Bulgarians are like,

00:25:35.091 --> 00:25:37.224
lot of Gypsies man, you know?

00:25:37.224 --> 00:25:39.836
Be very careful, somebody might kill you.

00:25:39.836 --> 00:25:41.142
(audience laughing)

00:25:41.142 --> 00:25:44.134
And I entered Bulgaria the
same story all over again.

00:25:44.134 --> 00:25:47.792
Amazing experiences, people
were just extremely friendly.

00:25:47.792 --> 00:25:50.439
And then I was driving
with one truck driver,

00:25:50.439 --> 00:25:54.325
going towards Turkey and I was
telling him the same story,

00:25:54.325 --> 00:25:56.145
how Croatians warn me about Serbia,

00:25:56.145 --> 00:25:59.263
and Serbians about Bulgarians,
and he's like ah brother,

00:25:59.263 --> 00:26:01.422
you know, that's complete nonsense.

00:26:01.422 --> 00:26:05.036
Croatians, Serbians, Bulgarians,
we're all Balkan brothers.

00:26:05.036 --> 00:26:07.752
But Turkish people, oh my God!

00:26:07.752 --> 00:26:09.964
You know what Turkish people are like?

00:26:09.964 --> 00:26:12.394
Very dangerous, somebody might kill you.

00:26:12.394 --> 00:26:16.242
Turkish people warned
me about Kurdish people,

00:26:16.242 --> 00:26:19.777
Kurdish people about Iranis,
Iranis about Pakistanis,

00:26:19.777 --> 00:26:22.389
Pakistanis about Indians.

00:26:22.389 --> 00:26:24.619
Indians didn't warn me about anyone.

00:26:24.619 --> 00:26:26.915
(audience laughing)

00:26:26.915 --> 00:26:30.958
It's like they're the last
frontier or something.

00:26:30.958 --> 00:26:32.499
Beats me.

00:26:32.499 --> 00:26:33.332
But yeah.

00:26:34.866 --> 00:26:37.481
It wasn't only a travel
lesson, but maybe a life lesson

00:26:37.481 --> 00:26:39.377
like not to trust all these horror stories

00:26:39.377 --> 00:26:40.607
people were telling.

00:26:40.607 --> 00:26:42.776
- And my last, probably most obscure tip

00:26:42.776 --> 00:26:45.280
is changing your relationship with money.

00:26:45.280 --> 00:26:47.373
I'm going to be a little bit harsh here.

00:26:47.373 --> 00:26:50.192
If the biggest problem
in your life right now

00:26:50.192 --> 00:26:53.561
is saving money to travel,
you are pretty damn lucky.

00:26:53.561 --> 00:26:55.732
Think about all of the other
problems you could be having

00:26:55.732 --> 00:26:57.603
in your life, family
problems, health problems,

00:26:57.603 --> 00:27:00.304
not having enough money to put
food on the table problems.

00:27:00.304 --> 00:27:02.540
Those are actual legitimate problems.

00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:04.654
Being bummed because you can't travel

00:27:04.654 --> 00:27:07.137
is a pretty awesome problem to have.

00:27:07.137 --> 00:27:09.791
And once you start
seeing it as a privilege

00:27:09.791 --> 00:27:12.442
to have that problem as opposed
to this crippling reason

00:27:12.442 --> 00:27:15.080
of why you can't travel,
you can start simplifying

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:17.055
money in your life and figuring out ways

00:27:17.055 --> 00:27:18.250
to get what you want.

00:27:18.250 --> 00:27:20.103
If you want to save money to
travel you have two options,

00:27:20.103 --> 00:27:21.742
spend less or make more.

00:27:21.742 --> 00:27:24.093
We talked about how to figure
out what your overhead is

00:27:24.093 --> 00:27:26.222
and your take home, so
start looking through things

00:27:26.222 --> 00:27:27.904
that you can cut out of your daily life

00:27:27.904 --> 00:27:29.820
so you can save more to travel.

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:31.860
And if you don't want to cut
out going out with your friends

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:33.808
and drinking lattes, then that's fine,

00:27:33.808 --> 00:27:36.257
but that just means now you
need to make more money.

00:27:36.257 --> 00:27:37.630
Can you pick up another job?

00:27:37.630 --> 00:27:39.540
When I realized that I want
to be able to quit my job

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:41.255
to travel the world, I needed to save up

00:27:41.255 --> 00:27:43.092
a lot of money really
quick, and I knew that

00:27:43.092 --> 00:27:44.512
there was no way I was
going to be able to do that

00:27:44.512 --> 00:27:46.015
with my current job as a surf instructor,

00:27:46.015 --> 00:27:47.471
so I picked up a bartending job at night

00:27:47.471 --> 00:27:49.510
and then I picked up a job
being a brand ambassador

00:27:49.510 --> 00:27:51.719
for a natural soap
company on the weekends.

00:27:51.719 --> 00:27:53.506
I was working all the time.

00:27:53.506 --> 00:27:55.352
Not only that, but then
I started cutting back

00:27:55.352 --> 00:27:56.998
on all of my daily expenses.

00:27:56.998 --> 00:27:58.443
I moved into a cheaper apartment,

00:27:58.443 --> 00:27:59.776
I stopped drinking and eating out,

00:27:59.776 --> 00:28:03.398
I did absolutely everything I
could to minimize my overhead

00:28:03.398 --> 00:28:05.120
and maximize my take home.

00:28:05.120 --> 00:28:07.108
If your dream is to
travel, then that's the way

00:28:07.108 --> 00:28:08.608
you can do it too.

